tsk export / tsk import: mbox-format patch series for offline transfer

Replaces the original 'tsk bundle' idea. tsk owns both ends, so we do not need git am — but we keep the wire format byte-compatible with git's mbox so it survives standard mail tooling.

Format (per task, one mbox file or one --stdout stream):
- Standard mbox 'From <commit-sha> ...' separator per commit in the task's chain.
- RFC-822 headers including:
  - From, Date, Subject: [PATCH tsk] <commit summary>
  - X-Tsk-Stable-Id: <sha of initial content blob>
  - X-Tsk-Parent: <parent commit sha or 'none' for the root>
  - X-Tsk-Namespace: <ns>-<human>   (optional, only if --bind requested)
- Body: commit message, then '---', then a unified diff of the task tree against the parent task tree (or against empty for the root commit). Diff paths are tree-relative (content, title, <property>...).

Commands:
- tsk export -T tsk-N [--bind] [-o file | --stdout]
  - Walks refs/tsk/tasks/<sha> from root to tip, emits one mbox entry per commit.
  - --bind adds X-Tsk-Namespace so the recipient can auto-bind on import.
- tsk import [<file> | --stdin]
  - Parses entries in order. For each:
    1. Reconstruct the tree by applying the unified diff to the parent task tree (use git plumbing; trees are small and structured, no working tree needed).
    2. Write the commit object with the parsed metadata.
    3. After the last entry, update refs/tsk/tasks/<stable-id> to the tip.
    4. Verify: SHA of the reconstructed root content blob must equal X-Tsk-Stable-Id. Reject the import on mismatch.
    5. If X-Tsk-Namespace was present, optionally bind under the active namespace (or the named one) — prompt or require a flag for cross-namespace binds.

Tests:
- Round-trip: export task, import into a fresh repo, refs match byte-for-byte.
- Tamper detection: flip a byte in the root content diff, confirm import rejects with a stable-id mismatch error.
- Multi-commit history preserved: edit a task twice, export, import, log_task on the receiver shows all three commits with original timestamps and authors.
- Namespace bind on import is opt-in: default import does not touch the active namespace tree.

Out of scope for v1: exporting queues, exporting properties indices (recipient's tsk fix-up rebuilds them), or batching multiple tasks into one mbox (can be added later — just concatenate entries).